## Implemented behavior

Retained Track2 sessions can now restore through a **fresh active generic experiment admission**. The path is internal/unforgeable: the experiment admission owns the DG writer token and routes the existing `SystemOptimizationSession` restore through that token, avoiding self-deadlock while preserving the exclusive writer.

The concrete runtime reacquires teardown admission before any retained lease cleanup for explicit reset, changed-workload rebind, process-loss Ending and runtime disposal. Lock order is fixed:

`Track0 global gate → specialized Guardian suspend/reset → DG experiment writer → retained Track2 restore → lifecycle retire/rebind → budget reset after success → DG release → Guardian reconcile → Track0 release`.

Tests prove a competing Track0 benchmark started during rollback remains blocked. Caller cancellation while waiting for teardown admission leaves the old session, retained mutation and exhausted budget unchanged. After admission is acquired, rollback/lifecycle cleanup is non-cancelable. If rollback fails, the old exact OS session and failed retained lease remain owned for retry and budget remains exhausted; releasing the failed teardown admission reconciles the specialized Guardian. A later successful retry restores state and only then allows budget reset.

Changed GameId rebind restores the old KEEP before exposing the new lifecycle. Loss of RunningProcess evidence after Active yields Ending only after protected restore/retirement. Runtime disposal now follows the same protected path rather than restoring with the specialized Guardian active.

## Boundaries and exact continuation

This closes the currently identified process-local teardown/orphan/deadlock boundary of the on-demand runtime. It does not create gameplay scene/mode/load/environment evidence. FF/FF MAX remain `CanaryContextEvidence=false`; test evidence sources remain TEST ONLY. There are no approved generic production candidate registrations, action→mutation mappings or budget defaults. External registry/vendor/driver tools remain outside the process-local exclusion. No real-game HIL/FPS gain has been proven.

The next bounded item3 increment is to consider an explicitly started/stopped continuous/scheduled coordinator around the protected on-demand runtime. It must be disabled by default, serialize cycles, perform no implicit startup discovery, and guarantee runtime reset/dispose on stop/failure. It must remain fail-closed/no-mutation while evidence/action/budget policy is absent. Automatic activation still requires production context evidence and Windows/real-game HIL.

Track6 item3 remains IN PROGRESS. Item4 learned action reliability and item5 post-session queue remain pending.

This documentary commit itself requires fresh exact-head Windows CI before being marked GREEN.
